          <!-- WHAT THE PIONEER CHURCH WAS -->
          <section class="section">
            <h2>What the Pioneer Church Was</h2>
            <p>The Advent movement of 1844–1860 was built by men and women who refused to organize into a hierarchy. It was a movement of the Spirit — not a corporation. The pioneers — William Miller, Joseph Bates, James White, Ellen G. White, Uriah Smith, John Andrews — preached the Three Angels' Messages and warned against exactly the structure that now bears the name "Adventist."</p>
            <p>Ellen G. White wrote in 1 Selected Messages 204: <em>"The church may appear as about to fall, but it does not fall. It remains, while the sinners in Zion will be sifted out — the chaff separated from the precious wheat."</em> The corporate franchise now bearing the "Adventist" trademark is not the remnant. It is the new organization of 1904 — the one the pioneers warned about.</p>
            <p>The three angels still fly (Revelation 14:6–12). The call to come out of Babylon still stands (Revelation 18:4). The corporate franchise does not change the message — it buries it.</p>
